3876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533876 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533876 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533876 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533876 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7df3e984-bbc8-5964-9312-1a2d645cc83b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533911 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533911 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533911 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533911 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5cdc719-89df-501a-9054-6f1aff3d4e0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533922 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c6a4769f-21c1-52e4-82b5-a8a4ff749939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533930 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533930 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533930 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533930 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533930 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d99708c6-cfde-5abe-b62f-e0c5ade1ed06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533978 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533978 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt{30}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14353486-3eaf-5d78-ba59-74f924378a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533978 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt{30}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose squares bracket $30$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt{30}$ lies.\nWe know that ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36$.\nSince $25<30<36$, we know that $5<\\sqrt{30}<6$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $5$ and $6$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.3}^{2} & =28.09 \\\\ {5.4}^{2} & =29.16 \\\\ {5.5}^{2} & =30.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $29.16<30<30.25$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{5.4}^{2}<30<{5.5}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n5.4<\\sqrt{30}<5.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{30}$ lies between $5.4$ and $5.5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533997 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533997 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533997 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533997 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{72},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{72}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533997 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e4b5639-d116-5036-9ab2-4b00a6b27dd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533997 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{72},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $72.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $64={8}^{2}$ and $81={9}^{2}.$\n\nSince $64<72<81,$ we know that the square root of $72$ lies between $8$ and $9.$\n\n![](images/e9/e931f5e15b35adb850966e40e4ad759c233d9419771998efba5dc91d9ab95bc1.png)\n\nSince $72$ lies between $64$ and $81,$ we expect $\\sqrt{72}$ to lie between $8$ and $9.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8.4}^{2} & =70.56 \\\\ {8.5}^{2} & =72.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $72$ lies between $70.56$ and $72.25,$ so $8.4<\\sqrt{72}<8.5.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $72:$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.4}^{2}$ is $72-70.56=1.44.$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.5}^{2}$ is $72.25-72=0.25.$\n\nSince $72$ is much closer to $72.25,$ $\\sqrt{72}$ is closer to $8.5.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{72}≈8.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534006 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{28},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{28}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5297aedb-eec0-5c60-a99d-266da204b16b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534006 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{28},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $28.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $25={5}^{2}$ and $36={6}^{2}.$\n\nSince $25<28<36,$ we know that the square root of $28$ lies between $5$ and $6.$\n\n![](images/9f/9f16093fe61b05142060e1dcc36cf824ec664b271499d24758831a6bc53757ac.png)\n\nSince $28$ lies between $25$ and $36,$ we expect $\\sqrt{28}$ to lie between $5$ and $6.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.2}^{2} & =27.04 \\\\ {5.3}^{2} & =28.09\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $28$ lies between $27.04$ and $28.09,$ so $5.2<\\sqrt{28}<5.3.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $28:$\n\n- The distance from $28$ to ${5.2}^{2}$ is $28-27.04=0.96.$\n\n- The distance from $28$ to ${5.3}^{2}$ is $28.09-28=0.09.$\n\nSince $28$ is much closer to $28.09,$ $\\sqrt{28}$ is closer to $5.3.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{28}≈5.3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534015 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt{88}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6581b3e4-1dde-5fc5-a59c-9d058401fe58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534015 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the two consecutive whole numbers that bracket $\\sqrt{88}.$ We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above $88:$\n\n$$\n81<88<100\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{81}<\\sqrt{88}<\\sqrt{100},\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n9<\\sqrt{88}<10.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that $\\sqrt{88}$ is between $9$ and $10.$ \n\nTo narrow this down to two consecutive tenths, we test the squares of decimals between $9.0$ and $10.0.$ Since $88$ is closer to $81$ than to $100,$ the value of $\\sqrt{88}$ will be closer to $9$ than to $10.$ We can start testing at $9.3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9.3}^{2} & =86.49 \\\\ {9.4}^{2} & =88.36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $88$ falls between $86.49$ and $88.36:$\n\n$$\n86.49<88<88.36\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{86.49}<\\sqrt{88}<\\sqrt{88.36},\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n9.3<\\sqrt{88}<9.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{88}$ lies between the consecutive tenths $a=9.3$ and $b=9.4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534084 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec99099b-9970-5301-8219-551157f9c01b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534084 #edb/ref 1074 "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt{45}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{2}=36$ and ${7}^{2}=49.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{2}=36$ and ${7}^{2}=49.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between $3$ and $4,$ since ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{120}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{30}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $6$ and $7.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt{45}$ or $\\sqrt{40}.$\n\nSince $45>40,$ we know that $\\sqrt{45}>\\sqrt{40}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{45}$ is the greatest number among the given options." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534103 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cffe8b2-9eaa-5609-8875-f9176136dbf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186533978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534104 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1186a56e-84b6-50a6-9e81-6065bd0a7d6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534104 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534107 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534107 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d3ce030-a464-5cad-80c6-c2925c812161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534107 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534119 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534119 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "463a2187-c3fe-599b-aa5d-f1c756b0cc9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534119 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186533997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534143 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534143 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad31145b-e901-58de-9d30-fd62353a75b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534143 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534084 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
